did people curse in the real deadwood  like on hbo's deadwood how did the movie  get made 13 years after the show ended  how did the actors handle constant  rewrites keep watching for all the dirty  details  david milch the maestro behind deadwood  came to hbo well into a storied career  he began writing for hill street blues  in 1982 beginning a two-decade run of  bringing his dense literary style to  crime and legal dramas his most  successful endeavor to that point was  co-creating the show nypd blue with  steven bochko by the early 2000s he was  ready to create a new show using law  enforcement as a window into the way a  community is formed but the original  inspiration was not the time or place  you'd think in a 2006 interview with  american heritage milch said my initial  feeling was that i'd set it in ancient  rome the time of the emperor nero but  hbo already had the series roam in  production which they felt covered too  much of the same ground so they asked  milch if he could explore the same  themes of order forming from chaos in a  different historical setting milch  realized that the city of deadwood which  circa 1876 was a lawless mining camp in  native american territory was exactly  the setting he needed  one of the most striking things about  deadwood is its unabashed vulgarity  milch didn't specifically set out to  dispel the genteel myths of american  western movies but hbo gave him full  freedom to use as much violence nudity  and foul language as he needed to depict  his vision of the west as accurately as  possible  welcome to deadwood the language  specifically became the most talked  about aspect of deadwood when it  premiered causing most viewers to  immediately wonder did they really curse  like that in the real deadwood speaking  with npr the curator of deadwood's adams  museum admitted the short answer is yes  deadwood was a very profane place there  is ample evidence that all the curse  words used on deadwood existed at the  time the show is set scholar and  dictionary editor jesse scheidlauer told  new york magazine the evidence that we  have is that they were using more  religious blasphemy than the sexual  insults which are popular today but  instead of using antiquated terms like  gold darn and tarnation miltenko went  with language that would be as shocking  to modern audiences as the citizenry  intended  if you're a fan of deadwood you might  think the first roll cass was one of the  more central prominent characters on the  show who will deliver the crudely  eloquent monologues of al swearingen who  will embody the simmering rage of seth  bullock or the anxious gentility of alma  garrett but a chance encounter in 2002  led to a small role being the first  official casting jewell the sweet  disabled cleaning woman at the gem  saloon as actress jerry jewell told  yahoo entertainment i was at horton and  converse drugstore in santa monica  standing in line this man turned around  and he said oh my god are you jerry  jewell it was none other than future  deadwood creator david milch who  recognized jewell as cousin jerry on the  facts of life a role which broke ground  as the first recurring role for an actor  with cerebral palsy he promptly asked  her to be on the show and after one more  meeting the first member of his  sprawling ensemble was cast  despite a main cast of well over a dozen  and hundreds of recurring guest stars  bit parts and extras no actor boosted  their reputation more during the course  of deadwood than ian mcshane as l  swearingen the owner operator of the gem  saloon mcshane was central to the  labyrinthian plot of the show but  surprisingly the role wasn't originally  written with mcshane in mind david milch  wrote the role for ed o'neal best known  as al bundy on married with children  according to o'neill in an interview  with eric hedegaard david milch was so  certain on casting decisions that he  assured him you're going to be my  swearingen milch was convinced that  o'neal had the dramatic chops necessary  to overcome his reputation as a  recognizable comedy star unfortunately  hbo hadn't been consulted at all on the  decision at that point the network  wanted to cast someone as relatively  unknown as the rest of the cast and  suggested mcshane as milch told cni  magazine the minute mcshane walked in he  just took ownership of the role in my  imagination  the most recognizable deadwood cast  member is almost certainly keith  carradine known for dozens of western  roles he was so well prepared to play  wild bill in deadwood that he brought  his own wig custom made for him during  production of walter hill's wild bill  speaking with the av club carradine says  he received a call from walter hill now  set to direct the pilot of deadwood who  asked him what his hair looked like i  said well it's short walter but i still  got that wig reception of his  performance was so positive that hbo  initially pushed back on the character's  planned death in the fourth episode of  the show matching the timeline of the  real wild bill's infamous shooting  during a hand of poker carradine  recalled his conversation with milch at  the time to yahoo entertainment milch  said the good news is they love the show  the bad news is they don't want me to  kill hickok despite the network's note  getting his hopes up for a longer run  milch won the battle and stuck to the  original plan the character's presence  aided by carradine's pitch perfect  performance reverberated throughout the  rest of the show's run  praised for its dialogue plotting and  depth the success of deadwood begins and  ends with the writing  could you have been born richardson  and not egg hatched as i've always  assumed  and while there was a large team of  writers on the show nearly every script  was heavily rewritten by showrunner  david milch to better fit the rhythm of  the language he heard in his mind  sometimes on the spot it wasn't just the  writers that had to swallow their pride  actors needed to adjust to the demanding  task of learning several new pages of  dialogue on the spot actor brad doreth  who played fan favorite doc cochran  recalled a particularly infuriating  incident one time he came up to me and  said you see this line and he gave me  four pages i'm replacing this line with  this and it was a four page monologue  well before game of thrones gained  notoriety for killing off main  characters deadwood by the virtue of its  setting and commitment to authenticity  was the best example of a show willing  to kill off major characters without  warning it was simply a reality of the  west and the particular power struggles  that came with being a lawless frontier  mining camp viewers unfamiliar with  history were likely shocked when wild  bill hickok was shot and killed in just  the fourth episode of season one plenty  of characters would succumb to illness  stabbings gun fights and more over  deadwood's three seasons and feature  length movie  run for your  life  speaking with inside pulse during the  filming of season two jim beaver shared  the anxiety that he felt over the  potential fate of his character  pure-hearted prospector ellsworth  everybody on deadwood is worried they're  going to end up pig chow well maybe not  everybody but i think the warriors  outnumber the non-warriors ellsworth was  killed by an assassin at the end of  season three  one of the most memorable moments in all  of deadwood is the season three fight  between dan doherty played by w earl  brown and villainous henchman captain  turner milt wanted the fight to be  completely realistic and avoid western  cliches or as brown recounted to slash  film no cowboys flying through plate  glass  so the actors and crew found themselves  coordinating a bare hand all-out brawl  in the mud of the thoroughfare with no  clue how to end it fate intervened at a  poker game brown was playing with alison  chains guitarist jerry cantrell  apparently his brother dave cantrell had  once been badly losing a bar fight when  in desperation he tried to push the guy  off of him he felt his finger hit soft  tissue and knew without seeing it that  it was an eyeball and so the makers of  deadwood found their way to have doherty  seemingly about to lose his life turn  the tables by clawing out turner's eye  in a stroke of luck and by far the most  intense visual of the show was born  beyond well-known old west heroes like  wild bill and calamity jane many  characters in deadwood were based on  real citizens of the historical city of  deadwood central characters like al  swearingen sal starr and seth bullock  were based on real people although many  characters had slightly different fates  than their real life counterparts the  show's legacy has driven tourism to the  real deadwood to such a degree that  there are many specific travel packages  designed around the show and movie  visitors can experience the deadwood  heroes and villains tour among others  you can even get a guided walking tour  from the town's first marshal or at  least a re-enactor playing the part of  khan stapleton who was also a character  in deadwood while they welcome visitors  the city's official website does have an  entire section of its website about  differences between the hbo show and  real life  in interviews time and time again the  cast recall their time on deadwood as  head above all the rest speaking with  the av club w earl brown recalled  something a fellow actor once told it he  said i don't really care about money  fame and that stuff what i want to do is  something that has an impact and  deadwood was that for me i think it was  for everyone involved something really  really special keith carradine remarked  to the av club as well that it wasn't  just the role of wild bill hickok that  attracted him to the park but the mad  genius behind deadwood that made it more  memorable i mean what a role to have the  chance to play but especially as written  by milch it was extraordinary stuff he  wrote this kind of american shakespeare  reflecting on returning to her role as  calamity jane for deadwood the movie  robin weiger told slash film we were  getting incredible words to say and i  knew that i was with an exceptional cast  that everybody i got to partner with in  scenes was humbling  wonderful after a slow motion death in  2006 when hbo and david milch debated  about production schedules costs and the  length of a potential season 4 for so  long that the cast contracts expired  there were rumors of everyone reuniting  for one or possibly two wrap-up movies  but each time the plans didn't come to  fruition it sank the sprawling cast  spirits further until it seemed all hope  was lost asked by vulture if he ever  thought the movie would really happen  the famously verbose ian mcshane replied  with a kurt no even w earl brown who for  years maintained a handwritten list of  every main cast member's commitments for  several years just in case lost the  faith at some point in 2019 he told  slash film i gave up beating the dead  horse after a few years finally on the  strength of deadwood's strong dvd sales  and streaming legacy milton hbo struck a  deal deadwood the movie premiered on hbo  in 2019 garnering high praise from both  critics and fans what a grand surprise  after such a piece of time  in 2015 david milch received a diagnosis  of alzheimer's just months later he  finally reached an agreement with hbo to  write a script for deadwood the movie  and began a four-year process of  wrapping up the story his usual mad rush  of reassembling sets and scenes on a  whim and last minute rewrites were  tempered by the symptoms of the disease  he relied more on the director and other  producers to run things on set and stuck  to the script but that script was still  pure milch magic and writing has proved  to be milch's best weapon to hold on to  his mind in the face of the disease's  progression speaking with vulture his  wife rita described it with an analogy  perfect for the legendary tv creator who  wrote an ensemble cast's voices like a  symphony conductor i compare it to a  musician who can still play and has  access to the memory of how to do that  and is still able to exercise his talent  the brain is david's most exercised  muscle  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

